ack and work  on the underlying implementation later  but you often don't get a chance to  redesign your interface because users  become attached to it even if it's not  officially anything passed to beta users  just get attached to things and they're  hard to change  so if you take us to the next slide what  we recommend is  working on the initial implementation  and interface together and then do some  iterating on the interface alone toy  around with it maybe in a jupyter  notebook play around with the api you've  designed decide if it's useful maybe  redesign it do that a few times before  you make your first release but there's  no need to do that on your  implementation is the point we're making  here it's about the interface that you  need to get  right okay next slide please  um so some other advice about interface  design  interfaces matter a lot so a coherent uh  lead is really useful having one person  who really sets the tone for what the  interface is like makes it feel more  cohesive and makes people catch on to it  faster  be careful about naming it's very easy  just to be sloppy about names and not  pay attention to things that like  functions that aren't present tense  verbs and things like that but that'll  come back to bite you later when you  can't break backwards compatibility keep  the public api limited at the beginning  you can always come back to that and  change it later but at the beginning  keep it simple  the helper functions are probably going  to change because you are  making the implementation a bit of a  hack at the beginning right  um and always remember that this is a  contract so beta interfa beta interfaces  might last forever even though you don't  want them to so just just be cautious  and always keep that in mind  and then the next slide please  after you have something working how do  you build and keep iterating um we'll  release often uh that's very important  because you get more feedback from users  more often use versio