ut your existing  functionality so not only do they see  the source code but they can understand  what kind of mindset what kind of  decisions have already been made in your  code and then they can know better how  their contributions should fit into your  code  next slide  and then finally issue tracking just  basic things of having issues to submit  bugs and enhancements and some kind of  progress tracker we used to use some  kind of kanban board  next slide  big thing users have a direct line to  your workflow  um similar to what ethan said before  you're not beholden to your users when  they request things or they submit  anything but it gives you a transparent  line of communication about  what they want what you're doing and how  what's being released and also if you're  using something like github you get  documentation of all those issues and  their solutions for future reference so  that way people keep getting bugs or  something else keeps coming up you have  that built in  uh next slide  all right so  um  so we have we've talked about tools how  to build how to document all that kind  of things but you need to maintain that  quality right if you just build  something and leave it it's not going to  mean anything um people are gonna stop  using in there and find something else  we're gonna build something themselves  uh so our two pillars of long-term  quality are responses and refactoring  you know you've got a user side and  development side  uh next slide  so when you're working with users you  got to remember that they are part of  your maintenance process it's not just  you and your developers it's the user so  you need to be responsive  literally respond to issues as they log  them just acknowledge they've been  received ask for clarifying details so  that way you can  um understand what's going on and  explain as you're implementing solutions  and have discussions about it and then  share progress with this as you're  releasing things so that way ev