ies that  supply products to kroger so you think  coca-cola free-to-lay all those products  that get sold in kroger and we do a lot  of the data science for marketing and  product placement within  kroger right so the company has actually  grown quite a bit over the last few  years we have  over 250 data scientists so  likely wherever you work that's probably  a pretty  large amount of data scientists for a  single company  that is rare  and the way that we work is we are  highly matrix which just means we have  data scientists embedded on teams  alongside engineers some r d folks  business consultants project managers  and so on and that's that's worked  fantastic  but with any growing company  we've experienced some some pains  as we've added more data scientists  more data science projects we've added  more compute environments we do operate  in  azure gcp and we also have on-prem  hadoop  and so with that what we have  experienced is a lot of duplication and  code and also code inconsistencies and  just some  some common procedures common code  procedures that we do throughout the  company  and and this is really what kind of  forced our com our our team to kind of  come into existence  and we spent the last three years  building out an ecosystem of internal  packages python packages  that serve to simplify  and make a lot of these code procedures  common across the different teams and  also to scale  the work so you can see we have a lot of  packages that are focused on providing  ease and data access  to be able to do  basic data wrangling aggregation some  modeling tasks  managing our environments and then also  some developer tools  and so  just as a real quick example like how do  these data scientists use these packages  well for a given problem for example if  a data scientist needs to  compute some  key performance metrics for a certain  product sold  in certain kroger stores across the us  they could actually take these different  lego  pieces or different internal python  packages  to do  parts of this task right so it could be  setting up their spark configuration in  a efficient manner  they could be using another package to  work with the different types of kroger  fiscal dates that we have  they may use another package to  be able to  identify wrangle and extract upc  information about a certain product and  so on so the idea is  that we have all these different  packages that serve different parts of  that data science workflow and the data  scientists can just kind of plug and  play with these python packages to  simplify their procedures  all right so  the benefits that we've seen i'm not  going to read all these bullets but  really it comes down to  faster innovation faster data science  development it allows them to scale  their work much quicker and better  governance so we have data scientists  that are performing common procedures  in a similar manner and we we can pretty  much guarantee when they use these  packages that they are doing it in the  way that's become accepted as a best  practice within the company  all right so what do we've seen with  this ecosystem well just to give you an  idea um we are well over 25 000 function  calls per month  and we typically have over a hundred  distinct data scientist users from  within the company using these packages  each month  so that's kind of an idea behind like  the ecosystem we created but really what  we want to talk about for the rest of  this presentation is what are some of  the key themes that we have found as we  develop this ecosystem that have made it  successful and that's kind of where  ethan and gus are gonna jump in so ethan  yeah thanks brad  um so we're gonna move through a lot of  these slides pretty fast there's a fair  amount of content but i actually sent a  link to the presentation to the chat so  if anybody wants to come back and look  at some of these slides more you're more  than welcome to um just a heads up as we  we go fast because we have a lot  um so how how do you begin to identify  what sorts of things you should be  building tooling around because you know  we're we're saying that there's a lot of  cases where you want the standardization  or you want simplicity but how do i you  identify the places where you can  actually um help the company basically  so our recommendation is really to look  for tasks that are specific to your  company or your team or maybe even your  industry um that usually means that  either there's a lot of business logic  involved or there's something about the  systems you use that is complex for some  reason maybe authentication  configuration  but we would warn you not to go for  things that are common problems across  data science  if a problem is common across all of  data science like a modeling pipeline  for example  those things are probably either solved  in common libraries like scikit-learn  and pandas or they're very difficult um  that would be why there isn't some kind  of tool that already exists for them so  be cautious about that  mostly you're looking for things that  are about your company or your industry  um you want to take us to the next one  brad thanks so some examples that we've  seen uh are things like internal sets of  rules that you apply to data sets before  you analyze them  so you can allow users to customize to  some degree if you want toggle things on  or off but basically apply what you  might call like golden rules for best  practice  um industry standard metrics so maybe  like median units sold per household but  excluding returns that's the kind of  thing that a retailer might calculate  things like that that are standard in  your company but  users have to write the code for every  time often  internal systems that require  configuration or authentication i  mentioned that before we use that for  things like spark apache spark and some  of our databases and then also if your  company has a fiscal calendar that they  use often and analyses work over that  fiscal calendar it's good to provide a  translation layer for users to go to and  from that calendar from the traditional  gregorian calendar  okay  next one brad thanks  so if um if you're working on this how  how do you think about the problem of  actually developing a tool and not  exactly from the code perspective but  just generally like what should your  mindset be  um so there's really two  there's scope build release we call it  and prototype and evolve and these are  two very different things you want to  take us to the next one  so in scope build release basically  you're concentrating on getting the  problem right and solving it correctly  the first time and that usually means  gathering a lot of info to begin um and  that means uh basically a lot a lot of  planning and something more like a  product  management mindset where you're going to  gather information figure out the right  thing to build work through it and then  release it to users and that's in  contrast with the other one  prototype and evolve  and in that case actually this is a  little more of like a hacker mindset  where you quickly tackle problems that  you see  you're willing to pivot and maybe not  end up solving the problem you started  on based on what's doable instead of  what is like high value for example  a big focus on the problems you can  solve and regularly redesign what the  product is um and that usually comes  down to something like interface  so we actually like that second one um  so if you take us to the next slide brad  thanks um  you find out whether the thing you're  working on is buildable much earlier  which is really nice because often we  have found that if you get lost in the  sort of product management mindset in  tools like this you don't get things out  to your users fast enough and you're not  able to pivot based on whether something  is doable and whether it's actually  useful  and also you should think about internal  infrastructure as sort of like lottery  tickets where some will only be used by  a few people but some will be used by  very many and by producing more things  you have a much better chance of hitting  on those lottery tickets  next slide please  so some some advice about using this  prototype and evolve mindset um try to  avoid getting lost in value  quantification that can take a long time  it does perhaps make sense with external  clients but when you're building  infrastructure for your own company  often that's very slow and prone to  scope creep getting distracted  um instead follow simpler heuristics  around like what do people find  frustrating at work just to solve the  things people find annoying  um that usually leads you in the right  direction be willing to work fast and  pivot fast throw together prototypes and  throw them away sometimes  and be willing to change what you're  working on if you realize along the way  that you have another problem that might  be easy to solve produce a tool for that  and publish that publish that and feel  free to not go in the original direction  and also don't don't try to integrate a  bunch of different teams needs into a  single tool sometimes that works but  often it's actually more trouble than  it's worth and you don't end up  producing something useful to anyone in  your attempt to make something useful  for everyone  next section please  um so when you actually dig into the  code how should you think about where to  spend your time and what sorts of uh  work should be done there  basically our advice here boils down to  focus more on the interface than the  implementation  this is a hacker mindset like we said  it's about throwing things together um  and it really doesn't matter if they're  fast if they're performant uh if they're  built in the optimal way under the hood  what matters is if they're usable um  they need to solve the problem and be  usable and that's really all you need at  the beginning you can come back and work  on the underlying implementation later  but you often don't get a chance to  redesign your interface because users  become attached to it even if it's not  officially anything passed to beta users  just get attached to things and they're  hard to change  so if you take us to the next slide what  we recommend is  working on the initial implementation  and interface together and then do some  iterating on the interface alone toy  around with it maybe in a jupyter  notebook play around with the api you've  designed decide if it's useful maybe  redesign it do that a few times before  you make your first release but there's  no need to do that on your  implementation is the point we're making  here it's about the interface that you  need to get  right okay next slide please  um so some other advice about interface  design  interfaces matter a lot so a coherent uh  lead is really useful having one person  who really sets the tone for what the  interface is like makes it feel more  cohesive and makes people catch on to it  faster  be careful about naming it's very easy  just to be sloppy about names and not  pay attention to things that like  functions that aren't present tense  verbs and things like that but that'll  come back to bite you later when you  can't break backwards compatibility keep  the public api limited at the beginning  you can always come back to that and  change it later but at the beginning  keep it simple  the helper functions are probably going  to change because you are  making the implementation a bit of a  hack at the beginning right  um and always remember that this is a  contract so beta interfa beta interfaces  might last forever even though you don't  want them to so just just be cautious  and always keep that in mind  and then the next slide please  after you have something working how do  you build and keep iterating um we'll  release often uh that's very important  because you get more feedback from users  more often use versioning uh  specifically we recommend semantic  versioning because you can signal when  you have breaking changes with major  version bumps  uh have a deprecation policy it's hard  to have one uh before your 1.0 version  but having something in place is useful  and be responsive to your users but  remember that you control vision not the  users and if you think something is  worth building and is doable you are the  only one who can make that call  if that rankles people who have some  sway  it is not the worst thing sometimes to  let something sit on the backlog for a  while um that's okay if if that means  that you can actually work on more  important stuff in other places  okay and then i think i'll pass it off  to gus for the next couple seconds  thanks ethan all right so we talked  about um  how we kind of look at building tools  and getting them up and running but  obviously there's more to  uh good tools than just that right let  me go next slide  all right so good tools are more than  just their code your ultimate goal both  in building tools and maintaining tools  is reducing friction and that goes  just as much for how you enhance your  code  if you reduce friction your developers  can more easily contribute and maintain  your package you get happier happier  coders and it makes your users more  independent and how they learn how and  how they learn about your package or  your tool and how they use it and it  keeps them off your back right if they  know how to use it they don't need to  bug you about every little detail  we go next slide  right so one of the big things that we  always recommend is testing suite  unit testing all that kind of thing  automation through ci cd  uh  contribution guide with instructions how  to use suite so that your developers  aren't just sitting there knowing that  there's a testing suite but they should  know how to use it to check their code  uh coming to the next slide  yeah right what does this get you  developers can easily check their work  if they have a testing suite  it makes it easier for them to check  what's working still and what they might  have broken while they're trying to make  updates  in general it helps you identify bugs  earlier  obviously testing is heavily going to  help your developers but it helps your  users in that  if you're identifying bugs earlier and  your developers have a way to check that  they're integrating your code properly  they get more stable releases and then  your users are happier they don't worry  about  bugs creeping in as often it can work  you know your api is generally stable  it just helps a lot  uh next slide  another big pillar that you'll see that  we like to recommend a lot is  documentation right for having a good  readme to having a change log to having  a contributing guide which you mentioned  before testing but  obviously that goes beyond just testing  and then having a website  with guides on how to install how to use  uh both at a high level and then a more  detailed level an api guide and then  especially for  when we're talking about creating tools  within businesses any business logic  that's not  necessarily obvious from your code or  gives more context to how why your code  works the way it does all that should be  documented  in the next slide  right  documentation as opposed to testing is  going to be very  helpful to your users because they can  get started quickly independently and it  gives them a good place to go first with  the question if they can go to your  documentation answer the majority of  their questions  they don't bug you with it they can and  you can get all their common questions  answered and then they can come to you  when they have something deeper  to ask about or something that isn't  quite clear in documentation or a bug  report  and on the development side  if there's a contribution guide and an  api guide  new developers can learn how to get  started with contributing and also they  can learn about your existing  functionality so not only do they see  the source code but they can understand  what kind of mindset what kind of  decisions have already been made in your  code and then they can know better how  their contributions should fit into your  code  next slide  and then finally issue tracking just  basic things of having issues to submit  bugs and enhancements and some kind of  progress tracker we used to use some  kind of kanban board  next slide  big thing users have a direct line to  your workflow  um similar to what ethan said before  you're not beholden to your users when  they request things or they submit  anything but it gives you a transparent  line of communication about  what they want what you're doing and how  what's being released and also if you're  using something like github you get  documentation of all those issues and  their solutions for future reference so  that way people keep getting bugs or  something else keeps coming up you have  that built in  uh next slide  all right so  um  so we have we've talked about tools how  to build how to document all that kind  of things but you need to maintain that  quality right if you just build  something and leave it it's not going to  mean anything um people are gonna stop  using in there and find something else  we're gonna build something themselves  uh so our two pillars of long-term  quality are responses and refactoring  you know you've got a user side and  development side  uh next slide  so when you're working with users you  got to remember that they are part of  your maintenance process it's not just  you and your developers it's the user so  you need to be responsive  literally respond to issues as they log  them just acknowledge they've been  received ask for clarifying details so  that way you can  um understand what's going on and  explain as you're implementing solutions  and have discussions about it and then  share progress with this as you're  releasing things so that way everyone  understands what's happening and they  can get their new code faster  next slide  uh we also mentioned refactoring right  so we have the developer side of this  next one  obviously clearcode helps everyone  across every facet of development um you  can see maintaining implementing  onboarding encouraging outside  contributions having clearer code helps  across the board  next slide  please  uh so the big thing is to be strategic  you don't want to be planning  refactoring for every single cycle or  whatever work session you're using but  you don't want to be just sporadically  tacky on refactoring into pr's that have  nothing to do with it you want to strike  a balance on trying to get that work  done without taking up too much of your  time because you have other stuff to do  something that helps with that is if you  put  potential refactoring ideas on your  backlog then you have a quantifiable  work work item to come back to you later  um but however you want to do it the big  thing is do it in smaller units even if  you're planning to refactor a big  section of your package  do it in as small as you can do it in  phases all that kind of stuff  uh next slide  all right so kind of high level  takeaways um so  really the the purpose of our ecosystem  has been around  um addressing issues when we've seen  enterprise growth and really growth  amongst a data science team  so  as data science teams kind of grow  significantly large you tend to see  stove pipe processes code duplication  and code inconsistencies that happen  across the company  and trying to  resolve those we have found to be  extremely  easier by doing it through foundational  inner source packages um the inner  source tools inner source python  packages have really helped to kind of  simplify and scale common data science  tasks  and so what are some of the things that  we have found  um as we've done this uh ethan and gus  kind of hit on them  many different lessons learned um around  like how do we identify the problems  that we want to work on  how do we do early development which we  really focus on kind of quick prototype  releasing very early versus very large  monolithic projects  with a long-term release date  and we really focus on the interface  that's something that we have found to  be extremely important where  as we put out more of these inner source  tools  the more common that we have the  interface look across them um the easier  it is for our data scientists to really  kind of get up to speed and start  working with them  and then some of the stuff that gus  talked about  maintenance long-term support of our  packages  is extremely important putting the time  and effort into refactoring and also  having that relationship with the end  users we have found to be extremely uh  important and beneficial in the success  of these packages being adopted  so with that marcelo we will  just hand it over for any  questions sure thank you very much  that's going to be fine there are some  questions by the participants  and  ethan was providing some support  with them  dilip was asking about  what tools have you used to keep track  of usage and metrics on your created  packages  yeah so let ethan talk to that one  because he actually developed a  fantastic logging tool that we use  internally  thanks brad do you want to show the  final slide we have an appendix slide  that mentions some of the internal  packages we use and that's actually  mentioned so i i did respond in the chat  but just to mention that um  basically what we found is yeah internal  packages and tools uh often  are of interest like their usage amount  is of interest to leadership and to the  people developing them and we  basically went in with a goal of how  simple can we make it to add usage  metric collection um so we have a  package which we call component logger  but all it does is expose a python  decorator there's really almost nothing  else users will ever see of the package  but adding the decorator to functions  and classes in another internal tool  will  automatically do logging for them and it  handles complications like in what  platform are you running what database  do you have access to write the logs to  so it writes to different databases in  different spots  and then we have other tools to go back  and read those logs but it really is  mainly for  what you might call uh like telemetry  like it's just sort of basic information  about usage and when and who  and then i i think there's another  question oh sorry brad  no i was just going to say i i think one  of the important things to kind of call  out about that is that was some initial  logging that we did and i think what has  helped a lot is ethan turn that process  into a package  and so what that does is it makes it  easy for other teams across the the  company that are building internal  packages just to go ahead and throw that  in as a dependency and they pretty much  automatically start logging themselves  so we made it very easy to do logging by  turning that procedure into an internal  package  yeah and that that's actually a bit of a  callback to some of the other stuff we  talked about during the session where  interface is so important compared to  the implementation like originally that  was a total hack but we did give people  a really easy simple interface to use  and by providing something like that we  got initial users and then we were able  to improve the underlying functionality  over time we didn't have to do it all  from the very beginning  the other question uh gus did you see  that one i think that would be a good  one for you about  philosophies or rules of thumb around  deprecating libraries and functions  yeah um  so obviously um we talked about throwing  away tools we don't want to deprecate  libraries that often but it comes up and  we deprecate functions a lot more often  the the big thing like we talked about  is communication  um so we do use our our component logger  to try to figure out how much people are  using  different aspects of our library so that  way we can kind of tell  without even gathering without even  having to talk to users and trying to  get their input we can kind of see  who's using what is something falling by  the wayside and kind of deprecate it or  if we're looking at making breaking  changes who's using it and  can we talk to them about new interfaces  or just make sure that they're aware  but the key to doing all that is  it's just communication um  we we try to  which even outside the application when  we're just doing releases if we're doing  a major release with breaking changes we  send out a lot of advanced notice of hey  this is how the api is going to change  take a look start pinning your packages  that way you can have a chance to test  it before everything goes into  production  um  we're going to minor releases where we  just start adding new features but we  want people to know about it so that  they're  not caught off guard by having this new  thing that could help them a lot um  we'll send out emails um  ahead of time and as it's released  um when you look at deprecating it's the  same kind of philosophy just  as much advanced notes as you can so  that people can adjust their situation  and when we have time we'll also  if we're deprecating for a specific  reason like to give it to give a new  package for that purpose we'll  start providing guides to  show how to convert your code from the  deprecated library to the new library or  if another team's developed a solution  that's a better use case  how to get to their library  um  it's really just being transparent about  it  all right so there are some other  questions from the participants  tim smith is asking how do you support  discovery of your tools among your  clients your client teams  yeah i think um  we are in a good position there where  we're kind of a team within the company  known specifically for creating internal  tools um so that's nice because we tend  to have a lot of touch points with other  teams and they do look at some of the  documentation we produce so they run  into these but also you can't  underestimate how many people are going  to just kind of tune out in a company  and not pay attention to that kind of  stuff  so really what you have to rely on is  that a few users who really like the  tool will tell their friends about it  basically is what it comes down to you  have to find like an evangelist user  base basically  thanks a lot  another question by one participant is  this  i'm developing an internal back cache  but i need to support python 3.6 to 3.9  so i was trying portrait to manage  dependencies  with back ported stuff like data  database classes  and a recommendation on supporting a  wide range of environments  did somebody else want to take that one  yeah i mean i think uh  i don't think we have a a great response  to that we do have a few different  environments uh like internally on our  on-prem system i think we're in like a  three-six  maybe a three-seven um and then  we've got what three-eighths in a cloud  environment  the main thing i think that we do  to  get around that is we  we don't look to  put in like the latest and greatest  syntax adoptions within a release  we try to keep it simple something that  is across the different environments  we are starting now to get into the  process of trying to do testing like  when we do our unit tests we will  actually try to run them across  different python versions that's  something that we we've actually just  recently started  talking about more and started doing so  it's something that that is something we  are working through  and i think that the main thing to think  about is one how can you do testing to  test across the different environments  that you have and then two  don't necessarily get to get wild and  crazy on adopting like the latest and  greatest syntax changes within a single  uh  environment  or version change  yeah and one very small thing i would  add to that um  some good guidance is test on the lowest  version of python anyone's going to use  run your tests on that and  run your type checking on a newer  version if you're going to use type  checking because you've got a lot of  conveniences and type checking in the  newer versions of python and you could  be more expressive and get better checks  out of my pie  but what matters is that the code  imports and runs not necessarily that  the type checking works but that the  code imports and runs in the oldest  version of python and that usually means  you'll be fine all the way up to the  newest as long as that's like after  python 3 basically so if you follow that  advice you're probably going to be fine  and those things pass you're probably  going to be fine 95 of the time  all right thank you very much that's  another question from raju  he's asking  well actually uh it's a set of questions  but i will mention them in ones the  first one is did you face any roadblocks  or communicate incoming or communicating  to management right  management regarding setting this up  since setting this up is kind of non  billable work  management or  executive team might not see the value  in it  that's the question from radio  yeah i know this is a good question um  so i would say  we did not necessarily get a lot of  roadblocks um initially from management  they realized that we had  inconsistencies across many teams  and they they really gave us some  freedom in trying to address that  but i think what was important early on  was we showed some quick wins  and we had a few different tools and  packages that got adopted  um significantly and quickly across a  lot of teams  and i think that made management  understand just like the scale at which  we can improve performance and uh and  code consistency across the company and  i think once we got those early quick  wins um it was  it was pretty simple to sell the need of  new tools when they came up  all right  thank you very much  well  i think  there are no more questions by the  participants  many of them are thanking you all of  three very much dilip i want to uh thank  you for your time and your amazing  presentation i actually enjoy  having a session of a full team a full  data science scientist team right like  working together in order to follow  their job experiences as companies are  following today right  so  i want to also thank all attendants who  participated with their questions it was  such a wonderful experience
 

